reliability (Admiralty source grading, of the source, not the claim): a_reliable (proven accurate), b_usually_reliable (established outlets, audited filings), c_fairly_reliable (trade newsletters, a mixed record), d_not_usually_reliable (a history of errors), e_unreliable (shown wrong), f_cannot_judge (new or unknown). The 1 to 6 credibility scale is not used; that an issuer speaks for itself is put_as stated, not a grade.
First value, from this table only, and stated in your reply for every new source, own notes included:
a document published by a public body, registry or regulator: a_reliable;
a company's own annual report, accounts, filing, release or news page, for facts about itself: b_usually_reliable;
the owner's own knowledge, or a document they described but you have not seen: b_usually_reliable;
the owner's note of what someone told them: f_cannot_judge (it records the call, not the speaker);
everything else: f_cannot_judge.
Every later change is the owner's (rule/independence, Revisions and regrades).
